15:08:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Right, it's essentially systemic rather than occasional. A few reasons it's so consistently bad everywhere: **The bandwidth limitation is baked into the phone network itself** Even with all-digital VoIP systems today, there's often backward compatibility with traditional phone network standards, or codecs are chosen for voice efficiency, not fidelity. So even "modern" phone systems frequently inherit these same constraints. **It's a solved-and-forgotten problem** The technology to fix this exists—wideband audio codecs (like G.722) can carry much better frequency range and quality. But adoption requires: - Both endpoints (the phone company's equipment and the business's PBX) supporting it - Actual configuration effort - Someone caring enough to prioritize it Since basically no one complains about hold music quality as a business-critical issue, that upgrade path rarely gets taken. **Everyone's copying the same broken template** Most business phone systems come from a handful of major vendors (Cisco, Avaya, various VoIP providers, etc.). If the default configurations and audio pathways in these systems are subpar, that mediocrity gets replicated across virtually every business that buys them, regardless of industry or company size. **No competitive pressure** You don't switch banks or cancel your insurance because their hold music sounds bad. There's zero market incentive for companies to invest in fixing something customers tolerate but don't act on. So yes—you're hearing the predictable result of an industry-wide technical debt problem that nobody has financial motivation to solve. It's not that they can't make it sound better; it's that essentially nobody has decided it's worth the effort.
